          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em salamander lemma
            is a fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lemma"} lemma
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            providing information on the relation between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            at different positions in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
          %p
            By a simple consequence illustrated in remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtramuralMapsAsDiagonals"}
            below, all the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing+lemmas"} diagram chasing lemmas
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            are direct and transparent consequences of the salamander lemma, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3x3+lemma"} 3x3 lemma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/four+lemma"} four lemma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/five+lemma"} five lemma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/snake+lemma"} snake lemma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequence+in+cohomology"} long exact sequence in cohomology
            corresponding to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
          %p
            These lemmas are all classical, but their traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
            are, while elementary, not very illuminating. The Salamander lemma serves to make the mechanism behind these lemmas more transparent and also to make evident a host of further lemmas of this kind not traditionally considered, such as an
            %a{:href => "#nxnLemma"} nxn lemma
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
          %h2#TheSalamanderLemma The Salamander lemma
          %p
            The Salamander lemma, prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SalamanderLemma"}
            below, is a statement about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exactness
            of a sequence naturally associated with any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Preliminaries"} Preliminaries
            we first introduce this sequence itself.
          %h3#Preliminaries Preliminaries
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (general assumption/convention)
            %p
              As always in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              in the ambient
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              it is either assumed that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              is of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
              for some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %em
                %a{:href => "abelian+category#EmbeddingTheorems"} embedding theorems
              has been used to embed it into such, by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exact functor
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              underlying these
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            %p
              In the following, many of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
              are spelled out in terms of elements this way, and we will not always repeat this assumption. This should help to amplify how utterly elementary the salamander lemma is. But explicitly element-free/general abstract proofs can of course be given without much more effort, too, see
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Wise"} Wise
