            %p
              This means that in order to get “far diagonal identifications” of homology groups in a double complex, all one needs is sufficient conditions that all the intramural and extramural maps in a “long salamander” like this are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            %p
              These turn out to be certain exactness conditions to be checked/imposed locally at each of the positions involved in a long salamander like this discussed in
              %em
                %a{:href => "#IntraExtramuralIsomorphisms"} Intramural and extramural isomorphism
              below. All the long diagonal identifications of the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram+chasing+lemmas"} diagram chasing lemmas
              follows by piecing together such long salamanders. This is discussed in the
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Implications"} Implications
              below.
          #OmittingIntramuralMaps.num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Moreover, it is useful to combine the extramural notation of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExtramuralMapsAsDiagonals"}
              with the evident diagonal notation for the intramural maps, lemma
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Intramural"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
